  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1133 AM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThe long term forecast for the Intermountain West is rather boring \nto say the least. Upper level ridging will put our region in a \nsubsequent chokehold for the next 7 days. This is great news \nfor anyone that needs to build a house or pour concrete this \nweek. Sunday, looks to be the warmest day of the week in the \nlong term forecast when the ridge looks like an amplified sine \nwave that advects in warmer but drier air into the region. High \ntemperatures look to be in the mid 60's for areas east of I-25 \nwith the potential of some areas to reach 70. 700mb temperatures\nlook to peak around 6 or 7C which will also give us slightly \nwarmer temperatures in the upper 30s overnight compared to the \nupper 20s we had the past several days. During the next work \nweek, there will be multiple low pressure system moving up and \nover our ridge dragging some colder air with it. While these \nsystems don't seem to currently present our region with \nprecipitation chances. It will knock our high temperatures down \nto the more seasonable mid 50's to low 60's and overnight lows \ninto the upper 20's to low 30's as 700mb temperatures hover \nbetween 0 and 1C.",
